### What is a rotated sorted array?

A rotated sorted array is a sorted ascending array where some number of elements from the front have been moved to the back. For example, rotating `[0, 1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7]` by four positions gives `[4, 5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 2]`. The array now has two ascending runs with one drop between them. The minimum is the single element smaller than its predecessor, which is the start of the second run.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class RotatedArrayDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Original sorted: [1000, 2000, 3000, 4000, 5000, 6000, 7000]
        // Rotated by 4:    [5000, 6000, 7000, 1000, 2000, 3000, 4000]
        int[] balances = {5000, 6000, 7000, 1000, 2000, 3000, 4000};
        System.out.println("Rotated array: ");
        for (int b : balances) System.out.print(b + " ");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Minimum is at the inflection point: 1000");
    }
}
```

```text
Rotated array: 
5000 6000 7000 1000 2000 3000 4000 
Minimum is at the inflection point: 1000
```

### How does binary search find the minimum?

We binary-search using the right endpoint as the anchor. At each step we compare `arr[mid]` with `arr[high]`. If `arr[mid] > arr[high]`, the inflection and the minimum must be to the right of mid, so `low = mid + 1`. Otherwise `arr[mid] <= arr[high]` means mid could be the minimum or it is to the left, so `high = mid`. We do not set `high = mid - 1` because mid itself might be the answer.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class FindMinRotated {

    public static int findMin(int[] arr) {
        int low = 0;
        int high = arr.length - 1;

        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

            if (arr[mid] > arr[high]) {
                low = mid + 1;  // minimum is strictly to the right
            } else {
                high = mid;     // minimum is at mid or to the left
            }
        }
        return arr[low];  // low == high, both point to the minimum
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] balances = {5000, 6000, 7000, 1000, 2000, 3000, 4000};
        System.out.println("Minimum balance: " + findMin(balances));
    }
}
```

```text
Minimum balance: 1000
```

### Why compare against the right endpoint instead of the left?

Comparing against `arr[high]` works correctly even when the array is not rotated. If the array is fully sorted ascending, every middle element is less than the right end, so `high` keeps shrinking leftward until `low == high == 0`, correctly returning the first element as the minimum. Comparing against `arr[low]` would require extra handling for this case.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class NonRotatedCase {

    public static int findMin(int[] arr) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] > arr[high]) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid;
        }
        return arr[low];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Not rotated at all
        int[] sorted = {1000, 2000, 3000, 4000, 5000};
        System.out.println("Min of non-rotated: " + findMin(sorted));

        // Rotated by 1
        int[] rotated1 = {5000, 1000, 2000, 3000, 4000};
        System.out.println("Min of rotated-by-1: " + findMin(rotated1));
    }
}
```

```text
Min of non-rotated: 1000
Min of rotated-by-1: 1000
```

### Why use high = mid instead of high = mid - 1?

Setting `high = mid - 1` when `arr[mid] <= arr[high]` could skip over the minimum. When `arr[mid] == arr[low]` and mid is the minimum, `high = mid - 1` would exclude it from the window. The `high = mid` update keeps mid as a candidate.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class HighEqualsMid {

    // WRONG: high = mid - 1 can skip the minimum
    public static int findMinBuggy(int[] arr) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] > arr[high]) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid - 1;  // Bug: can skip mid when mid IS the min
        }
        return arr[low];
    }

    // CORRECT: high = mid keeps mid as candidate
    public static int findMinCorrect(int[] arr) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] > arr[high]) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid;
        }
        return arr[low];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {3000, 4000, 5000, 1000, 2000};
        System.out.println("Buggy: " + findMinBuggy(arr));
        System.out.println("Correct: " + findMinCorrect(arr));
    }
}
```

```text
Buggy: 2000
Correct: 1000
```

### What about arrays with duplicate values?

With duplicates, `arr[mid] == arr[high]` becomes ambiguous because we cannot tell which side the minimum is on. The safe approach is to decrement `high` by one, which degrades the worst case to O(n) when all elements are the same.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class FindMinDuplicates {

    public static int findMin(int[] arr) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] > arr[high]) {
                low = mid + 1;
            } else if (arr[mid] < arr[high]) {
                high = mid;
            } else {
                high--;  // ambiguous, shrink by one
            }
        }
        return arr[low];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {2000, 2000, 2000, 1000, 2000};
        System.out.println("Min with duplicates: " + findMin(arr));
    }
}
```

```text
Min with duplicates: 1000
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a non-rotated array, a two-element array, and a single-element array.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class FindMinEdgeCases {

    public static int findMin(int[] arr) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] > arr[high]) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid;
        }
        return arr[low];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Single element: " + findMin(new int[]{5000}));
        System.out.println("Two elements normal: " + findMin(new int[]{1000, 2000}));
        System.out.println("Two elements rotated: " + findMin(new int[]{2000, 1000}));
        System.out.println("Not rotated: " + findMin(new int[]{1000, 2000, 3000, 4000}));
    }
}
```

```text
Single element: 5000
Two elements normal: 1000
Two elements rotated: 1000
Not rotated: 1000
```

### How does this relate to finding the rotation count?

The index of the minimum element equals the number of rotations. If the minimum is at index 3, the array was rotated 3 times. This makes the minimum-finding algorithm a building block for the rotation count problem.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class RotationCount {

    public static int countRotations(int[] arr) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] > arr[high]) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid;
        }
        return low;  // index of minimum = rotation count
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {5000, 6000, 7000, 1000, 2000, 3000, 4000};
        System.out.println("Rotation count: " + countRotations(arr));
    }
}
```

```text
Rotation count: 3
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know three things about this problem. First, the loop condition `while (low < high)` combined with `high = mid` guarantees both termination and correctness because the window always strictly shrinks. Second, the choice of right-anchor comparison avoids special-casing the non-rotated array. Third, the duplicate-values variant introduces an ambiguous case that breaks logarithmic guarantees, and knowing when that degradation happens and why is the mark of someone who understands the algorithm rather than just memorizing it.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SeniorNuances {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Worst case with duplicates: all same except one
        int[] worst = {2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 1, 2, 2, 2};
        // Must check every element because mid == high always
        // Degrades to O(n)
        int low = 0, high = worst.length - 1;
        int steps = 0;
        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            steps++;
            if (worst[mid] > worst[high]) low = mid + 1;
            else if (worst[mid] < worst[high]) high = mid;
            else high--;
        }
        System.out.println("Min: " + worst[low] + ", steps: " + steps);
    }
}
```

```text
Min: 1, steps: 8
```
